              program (https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng)
  ffmpeg      LGPL 2.1 or GPL depending on the build (https://ffmpeg.org)

ESP32-family boards (round 9 spike, round 10 emulator)
---------------------------------------------

ESP32 / ESP32-S3 / ESP32-C3 firmware runs in Espressif's fork of QEMU
(https://github.com/espressif/qemu, tag esp-develop-9.2.2-20260417), GNU General
Public License 2.0. Faber does not link QEMU: it runs as a separate program in
the Docker image built by toolchains/qemu-esp/Dockerfile, which compiles that
tag with Faber's patches toolchains/qemu-esp/0001-faber-bridge.patch,
0002-faber-peripherals.patch and 0003-faber-pads.patch (GPL-2.0, like the files
they modify; they add the GPIO/UART/I2C bridge, the peripheral models LEDC, SPI,
RMT, ADC, UART1/2, I2C on the S3/C3 and GPIO interrupts, and pad pull tracking).
Anyone receiving the image can rebuild it from the Dockerfile, the public tag and
the patches. A native QEMU built from the same sources (runtime "native") is the
user's own build, run as a separate program.
packages/sim-backends/src/qemu-esp talks to it over a socket (Apache-2.0).

ESP32 firmware (packages/sim-backends/fixtures/esp32*.merged.bin.gz and
anything built with toolchains/arduino-esp32) is built with:

  arduino-esp32 core 3.3.12        GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1,
  (esp32:esp32)                    Copyright (c) Espressif Systems and contributors
  ESP-IDF v5.5.5 (precompiled in   Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) Espressif Systems
  the core) and its components     (each component under its own licence; see
                                   the core's tools/esp32*-libs)
  esptool 5.3.1 (build tool only)  GNU General Public License 2.0, run as a program

The FaberEth Arduino library (toolchains/arduino-esp32/libraries/FaberEth)
contains ESP-IDF v5.5.5 components/esp_eth/src/openeth (esp_eth_mac_openeth.c,
openeth.h, esp_openeth.h), Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2019-2024 Espressif Systems
(Shanghai) CO LTD, built into the sketch because the precompiled core leaves
CONFIG_ETH_USE_OPENETH off; the rest of the library is by the Faber Authors.

Device network (round 10): the simulation build image faber/arduino-esp32:3.3.12-r3
(toolchains/arduino-esp32/Dockerfile.sim) adds the FaberSim library (Wi-Fi and TLS
shims for simulation builds only; by the Faber Authors, Apache-2.0) and installs
these Arduino libraries so the same sketch builds for the simulator and the device.
They are build inputs of the person's firmware, not part of Faber:

  PubSubClient 2.8.0 (knolleary)   MIT License
  MQTT 2.5.3 (256dpi arduino-mqtt) MIT License
  ArduinoJson 7.4.3 (bblanchon)    MIT License
  WebSockets 2.7.2 (Links2004)     GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1
                                   (a firmware binary that links it and is given
                                   to someone else carries LGPL-2.1 duties)

The device network itself (packages/device-net: gateway, netstack, DHCP, TCP, the
Wi-Fi control channel) is Faber's own code. examples/esp32-api-demo replays a
response recorded from the Open-Meteo API (https://open-meteo.com, weather data
CC BY 4.0) in network/cassettes/open-meteo.json.

STM32 and nRF52 boards in Renode (round 9 spike)
------------------------------------------------

The Renode backend (packages/sim-backends/src/renode) runs boards in a separate
process, the Docker image built from toolchains/renode/Dockerfile:

  Renode 1.17.0                    MIT License, Copyright (c) 2010-2026 Antmicro
                                   (https://renode.io; the image contains the
                                   upstream portable release unmodified; its
                                   bundled third-party licenses are under
                                   /opt/renode/licenses in the image)

packages/sim-backends/src/renode/external-control.ts speaks Renode's
ExternalControl protocol, written from the MIT-licensed client in Renode's
tools/external_control_client. No Renode code is linked into Faber.

Firmware fixtures (packages/sim-backends/fixtures/renode/*.elf) are built with
toolchains/arduino-stm32 (STM32duino core 2.11.0: LGPL-2.1 core, ST HAL / CMSIS
BSD-3-Clause / Apache-2.0) and toolchains/arduino-mbed-nano (arduino:mbed_nano
4.4.1: LGPL-2.1 core, Arm Mbed OS Apache-2.0), plus newlib / libgcc as above.

STM32 and nRF52 boards in Renode (round 10)
-------------------------------------------
packages/sim-backends/src/renode/platforms holds Faber's Renode additions, MIT
like Renode, compiled into the Renode process at start (never linked into
Faber): the bridge (FaberBridge.cs), a DWT cycle counter, an STM32F1 ADC, an
STM32F4 SYSCFG EXTI mux, nRF52840 SAADC / PWM / TWIM / SPIM / UICR models.
stm32f4x1.repl and xiao-nrf52840.repl contain copies of Renode 1.17's
platforms/cpus/stm32f4.repl and platforms/cpus/nrf52840.repl (MIT, Antmicro),
changed as their headers say. A native Renode 1.17 (FABER_RENODE_RUNTIME=native)
is the user's own install, run as a separate process.

Build images (separate processes; the cores are the person's build inputs):
  toolchains/arduino-stm32      STM32duino 2.11.0 (LGPL-2.1 core; ST HAL / CMSIS
                                BSD-3-Clause / Apache-2.0), linux/amd64
  toolchains/arduino-mbed-nano  arduino:mbed_nano 4.4.1 (LGPL-2.1 core; Arm Mbed
                                OS Apache-2.0)
  toolchains/arduino-nrf52      Seeeduino:nrf52 1.1.13 (Adafruit nRF52 core,
                                LGPL-2.1; Nordic nrfx BSD-3-Clause; TinyUSB MIT;
                                FreeRTOS MIT), adafruit-nrfutil 0.5.3 (BSD-3-Clause)
The XIAO nRF52840 simulator build (<sketch>.ino.sim.hex, and the fixtures
packages/sim-backends/fixtures/renode/*-seeed-xiao-nrf52840.hex) contain
Nordic's MBR and S140 SoftDevice 7.3.0 from the core's own bootloader image,
as the real board carries them. They are Nordic Semiconductor's binaries under
Nordic's own license (the "5-Clause Nordic License": redistribution in binary
form for use with Nordic devices); the Seeed core ships them without a separate
license file, so check Nordic's terms before shipping a product. The other
fixtures (*.hex) are STM32duino / Mbed builds of Faber's own sketches.

